"executive_summary": "This paper presents a field-theoretic reformulation of general relativity (GTR) in which gravitation is treated as a field living in a fixed Minkowski background, and then extends it to the Relativistic Theory of Gravitation (RTG) of Logunov, which abandons general coordinate invariance through a bimetric coupling. The author, Theo M. Nieuwenhuizen of the Institute for Theoretical Physics at the University of Amsterdam, argues that the two seemingly opposed views of gravitation—Einstein's geometric curvature and Maxwell's field in flat space—are in fact equivalent, and that the field-theoretic approach is itself equivalent to a curved-space description, so both apply, describing the same physics from a different angle. The gravitational energy-momentum tensor is derived from the Einstein equations in a straightforward manner, allowing them to be presented as acceleration tensor = const. × total energy momentum tensor. For flat-space cosmology the gravitational energy is negative and cancels the material energy, so the total energy vanishes. The paper considers the case of a positive cosmological constant and solves a singularity-free version of the Schwarzschild black hole analytically. In the interior the components of the metric tensor quickly die out but do not change sign, leaving the role of time as usual. The ΛCDM model is covered for cosmology, while a form of inflation appears at early times. Here both the total energy and the zero point energy vanish. The paper traces the historical development from Einstein's step from flat space to curved space, through the quasi energy-momentum tensor problem, to the proper energy momentum tensor derived recently by Babak and Grishchuk, and to the bimetric theory of Nathan Rosen and the Logunov school. The author derives the acceleration tensor and the tensor τ µν in Minkowski space, which coincides with the Landau-Lifshitz quasi-tensor for Cartesian coordinates and with the Babak-Grishchuk tensor γtµν/g in general. The Einstein equations are written in the Newton shape acceleration = mass⁻¹ × force. The paper then applies the theory to cosmology, black holes, and the motion of test particles. For RTG with a negative cosmological constant it was claimed that an incoming spherical shell of matter is scattered off from a black hole; the author reconsiders this and shows that the present theory with a positive bimetric constant regularizes the interior of the Schwarzschild black hole, so escape is in principle possible and the theory allows a proper black hole. The modification of the Einstein equations involves the cosmological constant, so it is of Hubble size and immaterial for solar-system problems. In cosmology the theory directly leads to the ΛCDM model, while it could accommodate a positive curvature-like term. At short times there is a form of inflation. The gravitational energy exactly compensates the material energy, and the zero point energy vanishes, though the cosmological constant is finite and positive and is canceled by the bimetric terms. The paper concludes that curvature is a geometric description of the gravitational field in flat space, that clusters of galaxies do not move away from each other but the speed of light changes with cosmic time, and that the quantum version of RTG, if it exists, will involve quantization of fields but not of space. The paper notes that topics such as a realistic equation of state for black holes, classical tunneling of its radiation, regularization of other singularities, as well as aspects of the inflation and of inhomogeneous cosmology are under study. The paper does not report a bibliography, so top_influential_citations is empty. The paper does not report data or code availability, so that field is empty. The paper does not report ethical considerations, so that field is empty. The paper does not report key figures or tables in a tabular form, so that field is empty. The paper does not report key figures or 